{
  "concept": "A graph with attributes is fully encoded for deep learning by three matrices: the adjacency matrix A (who connects to whom), the node feature matrix X (one column per node), and the edge feature matrix E (one column per edge).",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Panel label 'a)' top-left: undirected graph drawing",
    "Six circular nodes with dark brick-red outlines, white fill, numbered 1–6, arranged: 2 upper-left, 1 upper-middle, 4 upper-right, 3 center-left, 5 right, 6 bottom-center",
    "Seven slate-blue edges: 1–3, 1–4, 2–3, 3–4, 3–6, 4–5, 5–6",
    "Node feature vectors drawn beside each node as a vertical stack of 5 small rounded squares in orange/brick-red shades (varying lightness = feature values)",
    "Edge feature vectors drawn beside each edge midpoint as a vertical stack of 4 small rounded squares in teal/green shades",
    "Panel label 'b)': heading text 'Adjacency matrix, A' and dimension text 'N × N' in slate-blue",
    "6×6 grid of rounded square cells with column headers 1–6 above and row labels 1–6 at left; filled slate-blue cells mark edges (symmetric, zero diagonal), white cells with grey outline mark non-edges",
    "Panel label 'c)': heading text 'Node data, X' and dimension text 'D × N' in brick-red",
    "5×6 grid of rounded squares in orange/brick-red shades with column headers 1–6 (one column per node, D = 5 features)",
    "Panel label 'd)': heading text 'Edge data, E' and dimension text 'D_E × E' in teal",
    "4×7 grid of rounded squares in teal shades with two-row column headers giving each edge's endpoint pair (1/3, 1/4, 2/3, 3/4, 3/6, 4/5, 5/6), D_E = 4 features",
    "Highlight overlay: cross-shaped row+column band in A, column outline in X, column outline in E, glow ring on selected node / thickened selected edge"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "selectElement",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "That every node in the picture is literally one row AND one column of A plus one column of X, and every edge is one symmetric pair of entries in A plus one column of E — the picture and the matrices are the same object indexed two ways."
    },
    {
      "id": "panelView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Representation shown",
      "options": ["All panels", "Graph only", "Adjacency A", "Node data X", "Edge data E"],
      "default": "All panels",
      "teaches": "That each matrix stores a different, non-overlapping piece of the information: structure, node attributes, edge attributes — and none of them alone reconstructs the figure."
    },
    {
      "id": "guidedHighlight",
      "type": "state",
      "label": "Guided highlight",
      "states": ["none", "node 3", "node 5", "edge 3–4", "edge 5–6"],
      "default": "none",
      "teaches": "Walking through specific nodes and edges shows how degree appears as the number of filled cells in a row of A, and how edge ordering fixes the column order of E."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The graph itself",
      "narration": "Six nodes joined by seven undirected edges. Each node carries a D-dimensional feature vector (orange stack) and each edge carries a D_E-dimensional feature vector (teal stack).",
      "state": { "panelView": "Graph only", "guidedHighlight": "none" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Structure becomes A",
      "narration": "The adjacency matrix records only who is connected to whom: a filled cell at (i,j) and at (j,i) for every edge, empty diagonal because no node links to itself.",
      "state": { "panelView": "Adjacency A", "guidedHighlight": "none" }
    },
    {
      "title": "A node is a row, a column, and a column",
      "narration": "Node 3 lights up: its row and column in A show its four neighbours (1, 2, 4, 6), and its single column in X holds its five features.",
      "state": { "panelView": "All panels", "guidedHighlight": "node 3" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Degree is visible in A",
      "narration": "Node 5 has only two neighbours, so its row and column in A contain just two filled cells — the row sum of A is the node's degree.",
      "state": { "panelView": "All panels", "guidedHighlight": "node 5" }
    },
    {
      "title": "An edge is two cells and one column",
      "narration": "Selecting edge 3–4 highlights the symmetric pair of cells in A and the matching column of E, whose header names the endpoint pair.",
      "state": { "panelView": "All panels", "guidedHighlight": "edge 3–4" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Edge ordering matters",
      "narration": "Edge 5–6 is the last column of E; the column order of E is an arbitrary but fixed listing of the edges, and A alone cannot tell you which column of E belongs to which edge.",
      "state": { "panelView": "Edge data E", "guidedHighlight": "edge 5–6" }
    },
    {
      "title": "All three together",
      "narration": "A, X and E together reconstruct the whole annotated graph — this triple is exactly what a graph neural network consumes.",
      "state": { "panelView": "All panels", "guidedHighlight": "none" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A graph is not one array but three: A stores connectivity (N×N), X stores per-node features (D×N), and E stores per-edge features (D_E×E) — clicking any node or edge shows the same object appearing simultaneously in the picture and in a specific row/column of those matrices.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Four-panel layout: panel a) occupies the left ~45% with the node-link diagram; panel b) centre-right with the 6×6 adjacency grid; panels c) (top) and d) (bottom) stacked on the far right. Colour code is consistent and load-bearing: slate-blue = structure/adjacency (edges, A cells, A heading), brick-red/orange = node data (node circles, node feature stacks, X cells, X heading), teal/green = edge data (edge feature stacks, E cells, E heading). Nodes are white circles with red outlines and centred numerals; edges are medium slate-blue lines. Feature vectors are vertical stacks of small rounded squares placed just off each node (5 tall, orange shades) and beside each edge midpoint (4 tall, teal shades), with varied lightness representing values. Matrix cells are rounded squares with thin light outlines and small gaps between them. The adjacency grid has numeric headers along the top and left; filled slate-blue cells only at the seven symmetric edge pairs, all others white; diagonal empty. X has headers 1–6 above six columns of five cells. E has stacked two-line headers showing endpoint pairs above seven columns of four cells. Headings are set in the panel's own colour with the symbol bold and the dimensions italic below. Highlighting: selected node gets a glow ring plus a translucent band across its row and column of A and an outline around its column of X; selected edge thickens and pulses, with outlines on the two mirrored A cells and its E column; unselected panels dim slightly when the toggle isolates one representation."
}

